#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065250} :activity/id #uuid "073f76c5-241e-56ac-b230-c4ae3778c41c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362 :topic/id #uuid "eefa1995-be29-5c63-a8ec-6c5199131e6e" :topic/math-academy-id 337} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065365 :tutorial/content "The average rate of change of the function $y=f(x)$ on the interval $[a,a+h]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nThis can be interpreted as the slope of the secant line that intersects the curve at the points $\\color{blue}P$ and ${\\color{red}Q}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/32/3264f0792cee452453b024a61fb6f58a45290e133d3f84ec1ecf1c14501b4423.png)\n\nThe **instantaneous rate of change** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x = a$ is the limit of the average rate of change as $h \\to 0$. It is denoted by $f'(a)$ and given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, as $h \\to 0$, the point $\\color{red}Q$ moves along the curve $y=f(x)$ towards the point ${\\color{blue}P}$, and the secant transforms into the line that touches the curve at exactly one point $({\\color{blue}P})$. That line is called the **tangent line** to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point $\\color{blue}P$.\n\n![](images/37/3704b7e4f7a7aec6245d3494ef1e59a2b863e74a50bf5c9ecf471eec3789e7e9.png)\n\nThe value of $f'(a)$ is equal to the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f6bb245-1f46-5ade-84d4-6fd172b4b227" :tutorial/math-academy-id 322 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35e9e9bc-7d8d-5422-a6d3-4890eb3c5548" :step/math-academy-id 595 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064337 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063933 :question/id #uuid "7f0eeea0-e493-5287-9633-8128758d5d57" :question/math-academy-id "e-125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f2e684-6cd8-5ede-9f70-1a24ada54cb9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520476}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "28874baa-b4bb-54af-b29b-a7cbfc639bc9" :step/math-academy-id 596 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065366 :tutorial/content "Another way of defining $f'(a)$ is\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nWe arrive at this definition in the same way as before. But this time, we consider the interval $[a,x]$ instead of $[a,a+h]$, and then take the limit of the average rate of change as $x\\to a$.\n\n![](images/b4/b49c6bba1cb82227e7f47b2d1b91eae85ece94fd5251b79173842179cf7946e0.png)\n\nSometimes, this version is a bit easier to work with, so let's see an example of how to apply it. We should always arrive at the same answer regardless of which definition we use." :tutorial/id #uuid "6731f995-584b-5424-a320-c41c218307f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 324 :tutorial/title "An Equivalent Definition of Instantaneous Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "2fde7718-a29e-5055-884f-3f58ca08aaec" :step/math-academy-id 599 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065253}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064338 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063934 :question/id #uuid "95edcdc3-a675-5963-9e1b-e9eb0595327d" :question/math-academy-id "e-126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ce0d18d-f1ee-5654-984d-f191abc9a410" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520482}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point Using the Equivalent Definition"} :step/id #uuid "af0ad9db-1ba6-5216-8e5f-5d768427a09e" :step/math-academy-id 600}] :activity/title "The Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064428}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520540} :activity/id #uuid "a116c91c-02cc-5bc2-b27d-28406f69f493" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362 :topic/id #uuid "eefa1995-be29-5c63-a8ec-6c5199131e6e" :topic/math-academy-id 337} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "274df812-d124-5f50-84ac-b7aa8d336139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520047} {:answer/id #uuid "428ea4b9-5f3f-5c17-8bf2-2a02357d0d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520085} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9f2d30-713a-5a24-ab5c-b09f90073991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520279} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c75f0b-aea6-5e09-82a2-6259cf035e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520316} {:answer/id #uuid "fb249616-4f0b-5854-ba69-96ad9991479c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520316} :answer-field/id #uuid "26e0a8d8-3d22-5a55-a37b-cb46b000140b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7fa38cf7-46e9-5719-8fa2-6633394b103a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47887" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}$, the instantaneous rate of change ${f}^{′}(-1)$ can be computed using the limit of a difference quotient. The first few steps of this calculation are shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-1+h)-f(-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(-1+h)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{h} \\\\  & =…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinish the calculation, and hence find ${f}^{′}(-1).$" :question/worked-solution "By proceeding with the remaining calculations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-1+h)-f(-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(-1+h)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1-2h+{h}^{2}-1}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(-2+h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-2+h) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "2b61f38b-94db-5d5a-b982-f179ca27382b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12cc6b99-6aa4-5495-b69d-7c177d70318b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3(2)^{2}}{x-2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520015} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef965a1-569c-55ee-955d-a534fc912f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520061} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9b3af5-c3c8-599f-a61a-22160b2775b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x-2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520107} {:answer/id #uuid "896cf965-91e5-5f59-949b-28482f31bce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x+2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520194} {:answer/id #uuid "dddc1051-6963-51f0-a801-9b25da83d366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520107} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fdf64b2-7dfc-5534-bdd0-a30466c74ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "864d30c1-9b9e-5747-9ebf-06a4c172035c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47870" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$, what is ${f}^{′}(2)$ according to the definition?" :question/worked-solution "The definition of ${f}^{′}(2)$ is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}).\n$$\n\nLet $a=2.$ Then, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x-2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3af6a943-98b1-5bd0-aab5-0234441f6c41" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbcae67-db75-584e-abdf-39bdb2826748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520011} {:answer/id #uuid "16929637-3274-5e13-ac66-9758de4de287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520023} {:answer/id #uuid "478dcde0-ffcc-5e5b-9556-15d26a321acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520099} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed5baec-393d-5e5f-af72-0e15849dbfe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520138} {:answer/id #uuid "e70f5fff-9528-5097-9f8b-f32c917cacb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520138} :answer-field/id #uuid "140d3701-ba23-5c26-9cf9-6d695b5d1f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01c94ce2-04ad-5708-8ba5-8bfca9809325" :question/math-academy-id "q-101950" :question/problem "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=1$ by computing the limit of a difference quotient." :question/worked-solution "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5(1+h)^{2}-5(1)^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5+10h+5{h}^{2}-5}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{10h+5{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(10+5h) \\\\  & =10+(0) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)-f(1)}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-5(1)^{2}}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5({x}^{2}-1)}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5(x-1)(x+1)}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}5(x+1) \\\\  & =5(1+1) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a2d1e0d7-fe00-50a9-bcc0-d71842a58b46"}] :activity/title "The Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519992}]}
